[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]What is in the pack[/color][/b][/size]

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]UI Workbench: Inspect[/color][/b][/size]
Every ink layer of the game as a tree: the HUD with each of its pieces (the health bar, the minimap, the quest tracker...), the menus, notifications. Open any widget to see its children.
[list]
[*][b]Pick:[/b] point at the HUD or a menu. The widget under the cursor is outlined and named as you move; click it, and the tree opens down to it with its row selected. Only what the game draws is offered, the smallest widget under the cursor first.[/*]
[*][b]Right-click while picking[/b] sets the widget on top aside, so the one under it can be picked.[/*]
[*][b]Pick inside:[/b] with a widget selected, pick only among its children.[/*]
[*][b]Details:[/b] a widget's class, path, box on screen, controllers, the .inkwidget file it was spawned from, and every property, each part folding away. Copy its path or all of it as JSON.[/*]
[*][b]Filter[/b] the tree by name, class, controller or text.[/*]
[*]HUD pieces the game has not spawned yet (a boss health bar, a car's HUD) are listed and say so.[/*]
[/list][img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/3333/images/36958/36958-1793701413-1431326827.png[/img]

[list]
[*][b]Try:[/b] change a picked widget live, its visibility, opacity, tint, size, margins, place, text or font size, to find what controls a look. Nothing is kept: [b]Reset[/b] puts it back, and closing the Workbench puts back everything.[/*]
[*][b]To source:[/b] copy a picked widget and everything under it into a project of your own, as ink markup. The copy runs beside the original, a start for a panel that looks like the game's.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]UI Workbench: Edit and Projects[/color][/b][/size]
Write native UI of your own: a panel of [b]ink markup[/b], HTML-style tags the game draws as its own ([i]<column>[/i], [i]<text>[/i], [i]<image>[/i], [i]<list>[/i]...), and a [b]script[/b] that gives it its data.
[list]
[*][b]Projects:[/b] each one a mod of its own in your Documents folder ([i]Documents\REDnode\UI projects[/i]), safe from game updates and reinstalls. [b]New[/b] starts from a small working example: a slider that drives a HUD panel while you drag it.[/*]
[*][b]Edit:[/b] the markup and the script side by side, with highlighting, completion of the tags and attributes, and every mistake shown at its line as you type.[/*]
[*][b]Run[/b] shows it in the game at once; [b]Save[/b] while it runs shows each change; [b]Stop[/b] takes it away. A crash in your project never takes the Workbench with it.[/*]
[*][b]Export:[/b] the project as a zip that installs as a mod of its own, ready to share on Nexus.[/*]
[/list][img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/3333/images/36958/36958-1793701420-481148003.png[/img]

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Requirements[/color][/b][/size]

[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/35419]REDnode[/url] 0.56.0 or later, with what it needs (RED4ext, redscript).[/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/7780]Codeware[/url] 1.16 or later.[/*]
[*]Cyberpunk 2077 on PC, game version 2.5.[/*]
[/list][size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Install[/color][/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Vortex:[/b] Mod Manager Download, then enable and deploy.[/*]
[*][b]By hand:[/b] extract the zip into the game folder, so the app lands in [i]red4ext/plugins/REDnode/apps/[/i].[/*]
[/list]In the game, press [b]F10[/b] and open the [b]Mod Manager[/b] from the launcher (the [b]REDNODE[/b] tab at the lower left). Switch on the [b]UI Workbench[/b]; it starts switched off. Inspect works at once. To source, Try, Edit and Projects need its [b]admin[/b] permission, because they start and stop your projects and change the game's widgets: allow it in the Mod Manager's details for the Workbench.

[b]Uninstall:[/b] remove it in your mod manager, or delete the [i]ui-workbench[/i] folder from [i]red4ext/plugins/REDnode/apps/[/i]. REDnode itself stays.

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]For mod makers[/color][/b][/size]

Everything the Workbench reads is in REDnode's [b]rednode[/b] package, for your own mods: [i]ink.layers[/i] for the game's layers and HUD pieces, [i]ink.read[/i] for a tree of widgets in one call (with their layout and look too), [i]ink.inspect[/i] for one widget in full, [i]ink.at[/i] and [i]ink.boxes[/i] for what is under a point, and [i]ink.try[/i] and [i]ink.reset[/i] to change a game widget and put it back. Native UI of your own is written with [i]ink.load[/i] and [i]ink.parse[/i] in ink markup, or with the [i]ink[/i] functions. See the [url=https://edgemodders.github.io/REDnode/ink.html]native UI docs[/url].

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Questions[/color][/b][/size]

[b]Does it slow the game down?[/b]
A pick reads about 450 widgets in under 3 ms. While Pick is on, the boxes are read again every 1.5 s; moving the mouse asks the game nothing.

[b]Does Try change my game for good?[/b]
No. Each change keeps the value the widget had; Reset puts it back, and REDnode puts back everything when the Workbench closes. A save never holds a change.

[b]Where are my projects?[/b]
In [i]Documents\REDnode\UI projects[/i], one folder each; Export writes zips into [i]Documents\REDnode\UI exports[/i]. A deleted project goes to the projects folder's [i].trash[/i], where you can take it back.

[b]Why can I not click a HUD piece in the tree?[/b]
The game makes many HUD pieces only when they are needed (a video, a boss fight, a vehicle). Until then there is nothing to show; select it again after Refresh once it is on screen.

[b]Is it free?[/b]
Yes, and open source under the MIT license, in [url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDnode]REDnode's repo[/url].

